[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'If you want to use MiniMax H3 to generate a 30-second video, you usually have to generate it segment by segment, rewrite the prompts, manage the transition between each clip, and finally, manually join everything together . I tried several existing auto-chain workflows'

[00:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: ', but once I started adding precise action times, dialogues, and different scene transitions, the flow became difficult to manage. So I decided to build my own local director. It allows me to plan the entire video on a'

[00:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'timeline, automatically divide it into H3 generation windows, plan what should happen in each segment, connect them, and finally combine everything into a complete video. Today I\'m going to test it with a 30-'

[00:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'second story and a dialogue sequence, to see how well H3 can maintain character identity, environment consistency, time, and transitions across multiple generations. Let\'s look at the results first. Hello. Welcome to Smart'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Vision. On this channel, I constantly share the latest AI tutorials and practical tools. All the workflows, materials, and resources in this video are on my Patreon and are available for'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'download after you sign up. For followers, I offer additional professional tools and more detailed guides. If you like this content, please consider subscribing and supporting the channel . Now, let\'s begin. For the first test, I created a story that was about 30'

[01:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'seconds long. The scene takes place in a huge steampunk city. The main character first stands on a high balcony looking at the city, then leaves the terrace, walks down the street and finally heads towards a'

[01:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mechanic shop. This time, instead of writing a single long prompt, I placed each action directly on the timeline. This test actually checks two things at the same time. The first is the long video chain. I didn\'t'

[01:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ask H3 to generate the full 30 seconds in a single pass. The Local Director automatically splits the clip into safer generation windows. For example, this 30-second clip can be'

[01:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'divided into three 8-second segments and a final 6-second segment. Each segment is generated independently by H3, but subsequent windows continue from the previous content and the Local Director automatically joins'

[02:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'everything into a complete video. The second thing I\'m testing is action synchronization. If I simply put all these actions into a single prompt, H3 might not perform them in the exact order or time I want. The Local Director'

[02:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'first calculates which part of the timeline corresponds to each generation window and then only places the actions relevant to that time range in the prompt. Okay, let\'s see the result. I am the prettiest and smartest you will'

[02:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ever find. in the house of the patient wind and the blowing blood. We invite you to reach a completely new state of mind. To resolve the police war, this is what we did.'

[03:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'First, let\'s look at the character\'s identity . Here you can see that after the character turns around'

[03:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and moves through different parts of the scene, Min Max H3 still shows some noticeable identity deviation . At one point, he almost seems like a different person. However, the hairstyle and clothing remain mostly consistent'

[03:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '. Next is the environment. The overall steampunk style setting remains surprisingly consistent. From the balcony to the street and then to the mechanical shop, the visual style remains constant. The most'

[03:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'obvious problem appears at the end. Because this part is forced to connect with the last frame I specified, the transition becomes a bit abrupt, almost like a sudden scene transformation. In the future, I could add more'

[03:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'keyframes to make transitions like this more natural. Another thing that matters a lot to me is the transition between each generation window. If we simply take the final frame of a segment and use it directly as'

[04:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the first frame of the next one, we can easily create a pause at the boundary or make the motion suddenly restart . That\'s why the local director doesn\'t simply fast-forward through the last exact frame. Instead, select a rollover frame'

[04:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'near the end of the segment and then perform frame alignment during the final joining process. Judging by this result, the boundaries between the different generation windows are actually quite difficult to notice. Finally, let\'s look at the synchronization of the actions. The'

[04:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'character basically follows the timeline. First he stays on the balcony, then he leaves, walks down the street, and finally approaches the mechanic shop. For me, this is actually more important than'

[04:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'simply stringing the video together to get a longer duration. Because when I make a video with a true story, I don\'t just want it to be longer . I also need to control what happens and when it happens. Another great advantage of H3 is its'

[04:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'native audio generation. So I also wanted to try dialogues within a long chain. For example, the character enters the mechanic shop and says during the second segment, "Do you still have the key?" Then the merchant replies:'

[05:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '"The tricky part is that even when a specific window contains no dialogue, H3 can still generate random speech if the prompt isn\'t clear enough." In structured prompt mode, therefore, I separate the dialogue from the rest of the'

[05:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'prompt. A speaker is only included in segments that contain dialogue. For segments without dialogue, spoken dialogue is explicitly restricted. Do you still have the key? I would'

[05:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'leave things to Dasha. Yes. I saved it for you. As you can see, this 16- second video follows the prompt very closely . The characters\' actions and dialogues'

[05:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'also coincide with what I specified. The only minor issue is that Ollama accidentally added a bit of extra dialogue, but overall, the result is truly impressive. At this point, I can explain why I'

[06:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'finally stopped using the existing Auto Chain node. The node works, but when I need to manage different durations, start and end frames, dialogues, and camera movement, the node graph quickly becomes very complicated. So'

[06:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I decided to keep ComfyUI underneath as the model inference engine and add Local Director as a top layer to handle story, timeline, prompt scheduling, and long video assembly . ComfyUI is the engine. Local'

[06:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Director manages the production. Before we get into the workflow, a quick note about the MiniMax H3. If you plan to use H3 on your own,'

[06:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'especially for commercial projects or distribution, be sure to first check the current MiniMax H3 license and its usage restrictions. Terms may change, so I recommend checking the official license before using or redistributing'

[06:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'anything based on the model. Very good. Now, let\'s see how I actually use Local Director. You\'ve already seen the results of the generation. Now, I\'ll show you how I use Local Director to'

[07:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'create this type of long video. First , I create a new clip and select the model I want to use. Today, I\'m mainly testing MiniMax H3, so I\'ll select H3 here. Then, I set the duration. If'

[07:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I want to create a 30-second shot, I simply enter 30 seconds. I don\'t need to calculate how many H3 workflows it should be divided into, and I don\'t need to manually create multiple'

[07:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'build branches within ComfyUI. Next is the "prompt". Currently there are three modes: automatic , manual and H3 structure. In manual mode, everything I type is sent directly to H3. The system does not'

[07:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'rewrite it. This is best for people who already understand how to create "prompts" for H3 and want full control. Automatic mode is the scheduling system included in the free version. Use my own'

[07:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'rules-based planner, so it doesn\'t require an LLM or Ollama. Based on the video length, timed actions, dialogues, and scene settings, it automatically decides what content should be sent to each generation window.'

[08:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The third option is H3 structure. This is a professional role. Use Ollama locally to rearrange the H3 "prompt" for each spawn window based on the complete story, duration,'

[08:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'timeline, and even visual references. I want to make something clear here . Ollama is not necessary to generate long videos. The automatic scheduler in the free version can already divide a long video into'

[08:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'segments, assign actions, and handle basic prompt scheduling. The Pro version adds a local LLM to make prompt planning more flexible and reduce manual editing. The current default model is Gemma 2 2B. In'

[08:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'H3 structure mode, it organizes each segment into a more complete structure, including subject, summary , retention, detailed action, soundscape, and music. For example, in this steampunk scene, I can'

[08:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'start with a naturally written story . As a woman walks through a busy steampunk market , she meets an old merchant, receives a brass mechanical key, and then heads towards the clock tower. But the long video presents a major problem. We'

[09:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cannot send this entire prompt unchanged to every H3 window. If each segment receives the same story, the character might take the key too soon in the first segment, re-enter the market in the'

[09:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'second, or speak at the wrong time. That\'s why I can also add scheduled actions. Even if the 30-second video needs to be split into several generation windows, each window handles only the actions that belong to its part'

[09:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of the timeline. Next , I can choose a scene template. For this example, I can select exterior day, night, or another preset scene setting. This is very important for long videos. If the'

[09:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'first segment is a night scene, I do n\'t want it to suddenly turn into daytime in the third or fourth segment. I can also choose a camera setting here, such as static, dolly, or other camera movement, and then adjust the camera strength. This'

[10:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'means I don\'t need to manually type the camera instruction at each prompt. For image-to-video generation, I can add a starting image directly. If I need to control the ending, I can also add a final image. The'

[10:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reference weight and the weight of the first and last frame can be adjusted here. If I notice that the character\'s identity starts to deviate further down the chain, I can increase the influence of the reference directly instead'

[10:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of going back to ComfyUI and rebuilding the entire workflow. There are also memory profile and mode settings, which can be adjusted according to your GPU and available VRAM. Once everything is set up, I usually'

[10:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'click on "View compiled prompt" first. I find this function very useful because it allows me to see the final prompt that the director plans to send to each spawn window before H3 starts spawning. For example, I can inspect the subject definition,'

[10:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the summary, the retention, and the exact action that this window should complete. If there is a problem with a window prompt, I can detect it immediately instead of waiting 20 or 30 minutes for generation to finish, only to'

[11:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'discover that the first segment has already performed an action intended for the second. Once everything looks correct, I can click on generate clip. If it\'s a short clip, like 8 seconds, Local Director simply'

[11:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'runs H3 once. If it\'s 20 seconds, 30 seconds or longer, Local Director automatically splits it into several safe build windows, sends them to ComfyUI one by one, and uses a frame near the end of the'

[11:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'previous segment to continue the next one. Finally, it joins all the video segments back together into one complete clip. If H3 generates audio, the audio tracks are also preserved. From the user\'s perspective, I don\'t'

[11:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'need to know if H3 was executed three or four times underneath. I just need to tell the Director what I want on the timeline, a 30- second shot, and what should happen during those 30 seconds. Local Director is responsible for'

[11:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'planning the rest of the prompts , segmentation, generation, transfer, and assembly. Something to keep in mind is that Local Director is designed for workflow automation, not for accelerating inference. Model loading, sampling, LoRA, and'

[12:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'VRAM optimization are still handled by ComfyUI, so it won\'t make H3 generate faster on its own. What it saves is manual labor between generations. I don\'t need to go back after each segment,'

[12:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'take a frame, rewrite the prompt, and queue the next generation. Once the timeline is set up , Local Director automatically handles the rest of the generation, transfer, and assembly. Go to my Patreon to download and install Smart Vision Local'

[12:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Director Setup 0.1.0. exe. Then, simply follow the standard Windows installation process to complete the setup. After installation, you will find a shortcut to Local Director'

[12:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on both your desktop and in the start menu. Open it and you\'ll be ready to begin. However, Local Director does not include any AI model. It still uses ComfyUI on your local PC to perform the actual video generation'

[13:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '. Before using Local Director, you need to install and launch ComfyUI. When you open Local Director, the status at the top shows whether ComfyUI is online. If your ComfyUI instance uses a different port, you can'

[13:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'change its URL in the settings. Local Director can be opened even when ComfyUI is offline, but it cannot generate video. After installing ComfyUI, watch my previous videos on LTX 2.5 and MiniMax H3 and'

[13:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'prepare the corresponding models and workflows . Local Director does not download a second copy of each template. Use your existing ComfyUI environment directly. Remember that'

[13:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ComfyUI also needs the MiniMax H3 director node installed. Just click on download zip, extract the downloaded files to your ComfyUI custom nodes folder, and then restart ComfyUI. If you'

[13:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'primarily use MiniMax H3, I also recommend configuring the acceleration tools first. You can find the installation guides on my Patreon, including guides for Spectrum and Sage Attention. Long videos require many'

[14:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'generation windows running one after the other , so the generation speed of H3 becomes especially important here. If you want to use H3 structure mode in the pro version, you will also need to install Ollama. The automatic planner in the'

[14:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'free version does not require Ollama at all , so users of the free version can skip this step. After installing Ollama, run it and download the local model used by Local Director. The current default value is Gemma 4 12B.'

[14:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Local Director uses Ollama locally for structured prompt planning. Reorganize your story, duration, timed actions, and even visual references into a separate prompt for each H3 generation window.'

[14:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Once everything is ready, you can create a clip in Local Director, select MiniMax H3 or LTX 2.5 and start generating. After these tests, I believe the real challenge of'

[14:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'H3\'s long video is not simply making it longer. It\'s about managing each segment, maintaining consistency, and handling errors without restarting the entire video. That\'s why I built Local Director as a layer on top of ComfyUI.'

[15:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ComfyUI handles generation, while Local Director manages the timeline, prompts, clips, and overall workflow. It \'s still in an early stage, and the next things I want to add are'

[15:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'features like audio reference, character and scene memory, object continuity, and automatic quality control, while I continue to improve support for LTX 2.5. Ultimately, I want Local Director to become more than just'

[15:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a long-form video tool, but a complete local AI video production system. If you\'re interested in the AI ​​local, ComfyUI and MiniMax H3 video, remember to like and subscribe . I will also put the related resources on Patreon. Thanks for'

[15:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'watching, and see you in the next video.'
